\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The multiplicity action of charge conjugation after inversion}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Suppose the field space on a finite grid factors as
#{H_0\otimes E}, where the base #{H_0} is nonzero and both
factors are finite-dimensional complex Hermitian spaces. Let
#{E} carry the multiplicities. For the inversion and charge conjugation
of \ref{fdip-013T}, suppose the supplied maps factor as
##{\mathcal P=\mathcal P_0\otimes\bbI_E,\qquad
 \mathcal Q=\mathcal Q_0\otimes j_E,}
where #{\mathcal P_0} is unitary and #{\mathcal Q_0,j_E} are
antiunitary. Require the specified transformations of the base
field, represented group and backgrounds to hold and to keep
the two channel labels. On an
\vocabk{ordered Gram pair}{fdip-018P} represented by
#{\bbI\otimes A,\bbI\otimes B}, the composite #{\mathcal Q\mathcal P}
gives the destination multiplicity products
##{j_E A j_E^{-1},\qquad j_E B j_E^{-1}.}
For a fixed #{j_E} these two images must respectively equal the
specified destination products #{A',B'}. At a fixed Gram pair,
if every multiplicity antiunitary is admissible, the bounded
word-trace condition of \ref{fdip-018Y} for #{\dim E\ge1}
is necessary and sufficient for such a choice. On common
identified spaces the square of the full composite is
##{(\mathcal Q_0\mathcal P_0)^2\otimes j_E^2.}}

\proof{
\p{On a simple tensor the action of #{\mathcal Q} conjugates a
scalar moved into either factor. Hence it respects the tensor
balancing relation and defines a conjugate-linear map.
Inner products of simple tensors are conjugated, and expansion
extends this to finite sums. The tensor of the inverses gives
its inverse. Applying #{\mathcal P} first gives
#{(\mathcal Q_0\mathcal P_0)\otimes j_E}. Conjugating
#{\bbI\otimes A} cancels the base maps and conjugates #{A} by
#{j_E}, and the same calculation holds for #{B}. Tensoring
with the identity on the nonzero base distinguishes value
operators, as seen by evaluating on a fixed unit base vector
tensored with each multiplicity vector. Thus the two equalities are also
sufficient for the stated Gram comparison once the base data
are transported.}

\p{For any reference conjugation #{\kappa} on #{E}, the
composite #{j_E\kappa} is a unitary. Conversely every target
unitary #{u} gives the antiunitary #{u\kappa}. With all such
#{u} admitted, \ref{fdip-018Y} therefore decides existence
at fixed data. Squaring the action on simple tensors gives the
two displayed squares separately. A prescribed square
requires evaluating that product. To compare the original
maps one needs their source actions, and the full scalar
weight in \ref{fdip-011Q} also depends on its background
factors and insertions.}
}
